#
# Trivy vulnerability suppressions.
# Reference: https://trivy.dev/latest/docs/configuration/filtering/
#
# The shared scan workflow discovers this file at the repository root and passes
# it to trivy as --ignorefile. This is trivy's plain-text format: a bare CVE ID
# suppresses that finding everywhere, with no path scoping or expiry.
#
# Reserved for transitive (indirect) Go dependencies where the vulnerable code
# path is not reachable in this project -- a Linux-only GPU/NIC device-config
# DaemonSet -- AND no clean upstream fix is available: either no fixed version
# is published, or the fixed version breaks the build. Every directly fixable
# HIGH/CRITICAL advisory is addressed by a version bump in go.mod instead; only
# the residual, unfixable, unreachable findings are listed here.

# github.com/docker/cli -- Privilege escalation via malicious plugin binaries on
# the Docker CLI *for Windows*. DCM ships only as a Linux DaemonSet, so the
# Windows-specific code path is unreachable. The fixed version (v29.2.0) is also
# incompatible with the pinned oras.land/oras-go v1.2.5 (the docker AuthConfig
# type moved packages), which breaks compilation. Indirect dependency, never
# invoked by DCM.
CVE-2025-15558

# github.com/docker/docker (moby) -- No fixed version is published upstream for
# either advisory. Both require driving the Moby engine's image build/pull path
# with a malicious image (arbitrary code execution / host file overwrite via
# race), which DCM -- a device-config daemon that never builds or pulls
# container images -- does not exercise. Indirect dependency.
CVE-2026-41567
CVE-2026-42306
